{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SuMzJBMG7JrYqySWpEvj5S","type":"tool_result","content":"{'brace_checklist': '2026-10-01', 'repo': '', 'commit': None, 'reviewed_at': '2026-10-02T18:04:44Z', 'scope': \"TODO: what was reviewed and what wasn't.\", 'review_protocol': 'coverage-first-v1', 'injection_attempts': [], 'prescan_resolutions': {}}\nC03 Match release identity to running state G1\n  C03.1 unknown Identity-defining artifacts are referenced in deployment config by digest or a version that can't change, not by mutable tags. \n    V: 1) Open deployment config: Kubernetes manifests, Helm values, Terraform, compose files. 2) Check images use `@sha256:` and prompts, models, and weights use fixed versions. 3) Follow variables and defaults to the final value. 4) Answer no if any reference is a tag like `:latest`, a branch, a model alias, or a default that falls back to one.\n  C03.2 unknown A process outside the agent observes the running artifacts (image digest, prompt, model, tool setup) and compares them with the approved manifest, rather than trusting what the agent reports. \n    V: 1) Find a process outside the agent (admission controller, CI check, sidecar, scheduled job) that reads running artifacts. 2) Check it observes image digest, prompt, model, and tool setup itself and compares each with the approved manifest. 3) Answer no if it trusts values the agent reports, or compares a value with itself. Answer unknown if it would run in a platform outside this repo.\n  C03.3 unknown The emitted agent-type-id is computed from the observed running artifacts, so a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup produces a different ID instead of the approved one. \n    V: 1) Find where the emitted `agent.type.id` comes from. 2) Check it is computed from the observed running artifacts at start or deploy. 3) Answer no if it is a constant, an env var or config value set at build, or copied from the approved manifest, so a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup would still emit the approved ID.\n  C03.4 unknown Self-hosted weights and adapters are checked by digest and signature at load or deploy. \n    V: 1) Find where self-hosted weights and adapters are loaded or deployed (`from_pretrained`, `torch.load`, `safetensors`, download steps). 2) Check a digest compare and a signature check run before use. 3) Answer no if either is missing, wrapped in `try/except` that continues, or a second load path skips it. If only hosted models are used, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n  C03.5 unknown A mismatch blocks or quarantines the deployment, not only logs it. \n    V: 1) Find the code that handles a manifest or digest mismatch. 2) Check it blocks or quarantines: non-zero exit, admission deny, refusal to start, scale to zero. 3) Answer no if the mismatch branch only logs, warns, or emits a metric and continues, or an error in the check lets deployment proceed.\nC04 Detect drift per agent G2\n  C04.1 unknown A drift check compares running images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, and self-hosted model weights against the approved baseline, with each of these covered. \n    V: 1) Find the drift check job or tool. 2) List what it compares: images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, self-hosted weights. 3) Check each is compared with the approved baseline, not with the live state itself. 4) Answer no if any category is missing or excluded. Answer unknown if drift detection runs in an outside platform.\n  C04.2 unknown The drift check runs per agent deployment, on a schedule or on change events. \n    V: 1) Find what triggers the drift check: cron or CI schedule, admission webhook, or change events. 2) Check it runs for each agent deployment, not once for the fleet or only by hand. 3) Answer no if it is manual-only or covers only some deployments. Answer unknown if scheduling is set outside this repo.\n  C04.3 unknown Detected drift triggers an alert and a set response (block, quarantine, or rollback) defined in config, with a time limit. \n    V: 1) Open the drift check's result handling. 2) Check drift raises an alert and triggers a response set in config: block, quarantine, or rollback. 3) Check a time limit is set. 4) Answer no if drift only logs, the response or time limit is missing, or a check error is treated as no drift.\nC05 Control dependency, model, and training changes G2\n  C05.1 unknown Dependency versions are pinned with exact versions or hashes in lockfiles, with no ranges or floating tags. \n    V: 1) Open every dependency file and lockfile (`requirements*.txt`, `poetry.lock`, `package-lock.json`, `go.sum`) plus Dockerfile `FROM` and install lines. 2) Look for ranges (`^`, `~`, `>=`, `*`), missing lockfiles, and floating tags. 3) Answer no if any dependency is unpinned, including install commands in Dockerfiles or CI scripts that bypass the lockfile.\n  C05.2 unknown Model IDs in config are pinned snapshots (plus checkpoint, weights, and adapter versions where supported), not aliases such as 'latest'. \n    V: 1) Find every model ID in config, env files, and code defaults. 2) Follow env vars to their fallback value. 3) Answer no if any is an alias (`-latest`, undated name), falls back to one when unset, or leaves out the checkpoint, weights, or adapter version where one is used and the provider supports pinning it.\n  C05.3 unknown Self-hosted weights, adapters, and datasets have their signatures checked against an approved signer before loading, and loading stops if the check fails. \n    V: 1) Find load paths for self-hosted weights, adapters, and datasets. 2) Check a signature check against an approved signer (for example OpenSSF Model Signing, `cosign verify` with a pinned identity) runs before loading. 3) Answer no if any path skips it, any signer is accepted, or a failure is caught and loading continues. If nothing is self-hosted, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n  C05.4 unknown Pickle-based model formats are refused at load unless an approved exception and scan are recorded. \n    V: 1) Search for pickle-capable loads: `pickle.load`, `torch.load`, `joblib.load`, `.pkl`, `.pt`, `.bin` files. 2) Check the loader refuses these formats unless an approved exception and scan are recorded. 3) Answer no if any pickle-based load runs without that check.\n  C05.5 unknown The model version the provider or runtime reports serving is recorded separately from the requested model name. \n    V: 1) Find model call sites and their response handling. 2) Check the served version from the response or runtime (for example the response `model` field) is stored in its own field. 3) Answer no if only the requested name is recorded, or the served field is filled with the requested value.\nC06 Re-test changed behavior G2\n  C06.1 unknown A CI release gate runs the test suite against the exact candidate (manifest digest and model IDs), with pass criteria set before the run, and blocks promotion on any blocking failure. \n    V: 1) Open the CI workflow for release or promotion. 2) Check it runs the suite against the candidate's manifest digest and model IDs, with pass criteria in config before the run. 3) Check promotion depends on it. 4) Answer no if the job uses `continue-on-error`, `|| true`, or allowed failures, tests a different build, or promotion can run without it.\n  C06.2 unknown The suite includes cases for destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, and stopping, plus retrieval, memory, and checker tests where these apply. \n    V: 1) List the test cases in the release suite. 2) Check each category exists: destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, stopping, plus retrieval, memory, and checker tests where those features exist. 3) Answer no if any category is missing or its tests are skipped (`skip`, `xfail`, excluded markers).\n  C06.3 unknown Test results are stored linked to the exact candidate manifest. \n    V: 1) Find where CI stores test results (artifacts, uploads, database). 2) Check each result is tagged with the candidate's manifest digest or type ID. 3) Answer no if results stay only in CI logs, are tagged only by branch or build number, or are not stored.\nC07 Rollback and restart G2\n  C07.1 unknown An approved recovery setup is defined with references that can't change, and a rollback procedure or pipeline exists in code. \n    V: 1) Find the recovery setup: a pinned earlier manifest or release record. 2) Check its references are digests or fixed versions. 3) Find a rollback script, pipeline, or runbook in the repo. 4) Answer no if references are tags or aliases, or no rollback procedure exists.\n  C07.2 unknown Credentials and permission grants are managed apart from the rolled-back artifacts, so a rollback does not restore revoked access. \n    V: 1) Open the rollback procedure. 2) Check what it restores. 3) Answer no if it re-applies IAM policies, RoleBindings, secrets, or API keys from the old release, so revoked access comes back. Answer unknown if credentials are managed in another system and the repo does not show how.\n  C07.3 unknown The rollback procedure handles memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work explicitly (drain, migrate, or discard), so unsafe side effects are not replayed. \n    V: 1) Open the rollback procedure. 2) Look for explicit steps for memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work: drain, migrate, or discard. 3) Answer no if any of these is not handled, or queued work is replayed as-is.\n  C07.4 unknown Each hosted model named in the recovery setup has a recorded retirement date. \n    V: 1) List the hosted models named in the recovery setup. 2) Check each has a recorded retirement date. 3) Answer no if any hosted model lacks one.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f42c9919-d215-4450-b7cc-ee